#{[17592186455418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455418 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455418 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455418 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455418 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de328ef9-23d7-525d-a753-5f70c0536f52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455433 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}0x+0y \\\\ 0x+0y\\end{aligned}],$ find  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1489ce7f-875c-5e62-837a-1b4169461580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455433 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$ Notice that $T$ has $2$ non-pivot columns. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=2.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-4=2\\cdot 2-4=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455454 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x+3y \\\\ -12x-9y\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52f90c3e-788d-5e75-8c65-475f701ec88b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455454 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -72\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vectors that belong to the kernel of $\\,T$ are $\\,{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455477 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455477 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455477 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z \\\\ 6x-3y-3z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b821123-b07b-51e0-96de-b9776f35dea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455477 #edb/ref 1074 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{9}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=z.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx=2y-z=2z-z=z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455500 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455500 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ 15x+5y \\\\ -6x-2y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7ba141f-fb4c-5677-9c12-2cecf5acd474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455500 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+6 \\\\ 15\\cdot k+5\\cdot 6 \\\\ -6\\cdot k-2\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ 15k+30 \\\\ -6k-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ 15k+30=0 \\\\ -6k-12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455506 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+2y \\\\ -9x-6y\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5cb0f8a-8e07-5249-bc79-49a7b77ca413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455506 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vectors that belong to the kernel of $\\,T$ are $\\,{v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455510 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455510 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+4y \\\\ -x+2y\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f32f1d75-9b11-57ea-bfe4-1528ecbfd581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455510 #edb/ref 1074 "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y.$ From the second equation, we obtain $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $T$ is $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
